  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(2,{300}^{\u2218}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({300}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({300}^{\u2218}) \\\\  & =2(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(1,-\\sqrt{3}).$",
